<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Psusennes I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pasebakhaenniut I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Psusennes I was a king of the Twenty-first Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> in the late 11th century BCE for about 46 years. He built the great enclosure wall of the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at Tanis, within which he was buried. His tomb, found intact by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-montet">Pierre Montet</a> in 1940, held a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coffin, a gold mask and rich jewellery, now in the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/psusennes-i.jpg" alt="Psusennes I" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tjflex2 or (Craig Elliott) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Psusennes_I%27s_Funerary_Mask_in_2012_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
